[Applause]  please welcome nabin salem  i'm uh i'm very honored to meet you  i really love your story  how did you end up on this trip in the  first place so i mean really the reason  behind it is my dad and he's from  tanzania along with my grandparents and  great grandparents we have a long  family history in east africa and also  at the same time my daughters were all  about under the age of five and so i  packed my bags one day and went to  dharasalam tanzania to see what was  going on in the world and you know  wanted to see like what's missing what  what needs to be done what action needs  to be taken  and i started to learn about  malnutrition and  understood that 8 000 children a day  were dying from something that is 100  preventable which is hunger and  malnutrition  so just looking into my own daughter's  eyes right and understanding that  mothers every single day are losing  their children to something that we know  how to treat and i really felt that  urgency  because obviously i didn't have a lot of  free time at that moment with all of  these little kids to take care of but  that urgency really stayed with me and  it kept me up at night and i knew that i  had to do something about it and i  didn't have any experience i'd never  worked for a non-profit before  i have no manufacturing experience  nothing but i was like if we don't do  something then lives are at stake and  this is an opportunity and a  responsibility to do something at that  moment and take action i mean  where did you go  like  where did you start you know  did people take you seriously what was  what was your first  lesson that we could all learn from yeah  i mean i traveled with a bunch of  different non-profits and i learned  about their philosophies their mandates  how they worked and i came home and i  said i want to do this completely  differently and i remember having a  follow-up call with them and saying i  have this idea to start a business but  it's a non-profit but it's a business  and and so we can do something at scale  we can feed these children at scale and  they said that's not how it's done  and so for 24 hours i was really sad  that my idea was terrible idea and then  i thought to myself wait a second i've  only been in this business for 20  minutes 24 hours these people have been  in for 20 years maybe they've just been  there too long maybe this is the way of  the future let's give it a try so i just  picked myself back up and i'm like we're  going to go for it and we're going to  make this food at scale and we're going  to try to reach thousands and millions  of children and make it happen  so  you have helped more than two million  kids um last year  um  and  what now  is your goal  the need in the world has doubled so  what i need to do now is double the size  of our factory so we're trying to raise  money now to be able to buy the  equipment that we need to be able to  produce more of these packets i brought  one with me so we make a million five of  these every single day we work 24 hours  a day making this product called  plumping up  i have 110 people who are on my team we  work 24 hours a day to be able to  deliver these products to 60 countries  i'm  did you put money from your own pocket  into this endeavor yes yes but we also  you know  i take loans from banks i make  partnerships we raise money so it's  really a little bit of everything but  definitely there's a personal investment  as well  well we heard that purdue farms has  actually been a big supporter of yours  and we are thrilled to tell you um that  through working with them we've actually  um  yep here comes the big check we've  raised 25 thousand dollars  courtesy of purdue  and  [Music]  i um  uh chris miller my producing partner and  i want to match that also um so  thank you  thank you  so much for everything that you do  um  thank you oh my god nathan go to the  drewberrymore drewberrymareshow.com to  find out more about nathan's mission to  end world hunger and know how you can  help start today thank you  [Music]  you

